The 156 YAML files under hypergumbo ARE what it knows: which I/O primitives it
recognises, which framework conventions it enriches, which taint sources it
trusts. A user deciding whether to believe a verdict, or working out why their
framework was not detected, had no way to ask what their installation carries.

`scripts/yaml-catalog-index` does not answer this. It answers the OPERATOR's
question -- is the registry in sync with the tree -- and it ships to nobody:
pyproject.toml packages src/hypergumbo_core, so scripts/ is not in the wheel and
an installed hypergumbo cannot run it at all.

FOUR QUESTIONS, which are the item's and not mine:
  * what is loaded RIGHT NOW, including the community overlays and the user's
    own rows;
  * which families may I EXTEND, and where does my file go;
  * per language, is there a catalogue at all and is it finished
    (provenance_declared vs in_progress) -- today that reaches a user only as a
    one-line stderr warning during an analysis run, which is the wrong moment;
  * why should I care -- one sentence per family naming what changes without
    it, because "io_primitives: 15 files" tells a reader nothing about whether
    to trust a verdict.

EXTENSIBLE AND READABLE ARE REPORTED SEPARATELY, BECAUSE THEY DIFFER. Six
families declare a user_channel; exactly one, io_primitives.d, is scanned. The
other five are declared extensible with no loader taught to look (WI-sofov).
Printing "extensible: yes" for all six would describe an intention as a
capability and send someone to write a file that does nothing. That distinction
is PINNED BEHAVIOURALLY -- the wired one is asserted through
_resolve_io_overlays, and wiring a second fails the test until the inventory is
told, rather than letting it advertise a capability the tool lacks.

THE LANGUAGE LIST COMES FROM THE TREE. CATALOG_LANGUAGES names fourteen and the
package ships fifteen; bash.yaml is the one outside it, which is how it also sat
outside the WI-sugav subprocess guard (WI-surun found that). An inventory built
on the constant would under-report the installation it describes, so it globs
the catalogue directory and a test pins the two in agreement.

EVERYTHING ELSE IS DERIVED FROM YAML_CATALOGS -- the family list, every
extensibility answer, the channel directories -- so a new family appears with no
edit here and cannot appear with a blank answer: the per-family "why should I
care" mapping is gated by a test asserting it covers the registry exactly.

NAMING, WHICH THE ITEM LEFT OPEN. `catalog` is taken and stays what it is: the
languages and frameworks hypergumbo can ANALYSE. This is the DATA behind that,
so it is a sibling verb rather than a --yaml mode of a command answering a
different question. `catalog-inventory` is explicit and not confusable with
`catalog` at a glance; a test asserts both exist and that their help text
distinguishes them. Reversible.

